\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $\Lambda$ be a finite periodic square lattice with $N=|\Lambda|$ divisible by four. Give each oriented nearest-neighbour bond a phase $\theta_{xy}\in\mathbb R/2\pi\mathbb Z$ with $\theta_{yx}=-\theta_{xy}$. The Hermitian hopping matrix has $T_{xy}=e^{i\theta_{xy}}$ on these bonds and zero otherwise. Write its eigenvalues as $\lambda_1\le\cdots\le\lambda_N$ and define the quarter-filled energy $E(\theta)=\sum_{j=1}^{N/4}\lambda_j$. For each elementary square, its flux $\Phi_p$ is the sum of the phases along a consistently oriented boundary, modulo $2\pi$; bond phases also allow global twists around the torus.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Is the minimum of $E(\theta)$ over all bond phases attained by a configuration with $\Phi_p=\pi/2$ on every elementary square? The optimization retains all allowed global twists as part of the bond-phase data. \href{https://web.math.princeton.edu/~aizenman/OpenProblems_MathPhys/9802.OptFlux.html}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
For noninteracting electrons filling the lowest quarter of a periodic square-lattice band, does one quarter of a flux quantum through every elementary square minimize the total energy?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 7800012 (AMR-077-0012). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Elliott H. Lieb, Optimal Flux for the Quarter-Filled Band (15 September 1998), Embedded technical statement beginning Consider a large square lattice. \url{https://web.math.princeton.edu/~aizenman/OpenProblems_MathPhys/9802.OptFlux.html}.
\end{description}
\label{end:7800012}

